{"﻿original_id": "stim177_gpt3.5_A_1", "validator_id": "0", "prompt": "The topic at issue is whether Sentence 1 of a proposed solution to a problem is correct or flawed. A sentence is 'flawed' if it contains one or more claims that do not make logical sense, are untrue, seem true but misleading, or have other serious issues. Here is the problem and proposed solution:\n\nProblem:\nLojban is a constructed human language created by the Logical Language Group in 1987. Which of the following is written in valid, grammatical standard Lojban?\n\n(A) ku se ka vau kei stela le tunba steba\n(B) le ka stela vau kei se steba ku tunba\n\nProposed solution:\n1. The correct answer is (A) \"ku se ka vau kei stela le tunba steba\".\n2. This sentence follows the correct Lojban grammar structure, which is \"ku [sumti] kei [selbri] [sumti]\".\n3. In this case, \"stela\" is the selbri (predicate) and \"le tunba steba\" is the sumti (argument).\n4. The \"se ka vau\" construction is used to indicate that \"stela\" is the cause of \"le tunba steba\".\n5. On the other hand, (B) \"le ka stela vau kei se steba ku tunba\" is not grammatically correct in Lojban.\n6. The \"le ka\" construction is not a valid Lojban structure, and the placement of \"se steba\" before \"ku tunba\" is also incorrect.\n7. Therefore, (A) is the correct answer and (B) is not a valid Lojban sentence.\n\nIs Sentence 1 of the proposed solution correct or flawed?", "sentence": "1", "choice_a": "Sentence 1 is correct.", "choice_b": "Sentence 1 is flawed.", "correct_answer": "FLAWED", "original_key": "B", "explanation": "Sentence 1: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: False, because “ku se ka vau kei” is ungrammatical nonsense. It cannot occur anywhere in Lojban, even if you changed the surrounding sentence. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see following comments_\n\nSentence 2: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: A simplified but correct description of Lojban sentence structure would have been “[sumti] [selbri] [sumti]”. This is not the only possible sentence structure, but it is the most common one. “ku [sumti] kei [selbri] [sumti]” looks close, but “ku [sumti] kei” is not a valid pattern.“ku” terminates sumti (arguments) that are headed by the determiner “lo”. It does not preceded any sumti (arguments).“kei” is the terminator for various “abstraction particles”. It is out of place in this alleged pattern. -- Annotator 2 comment: {ku} and {kei} are terminators for LE (aka nominalizers, or 'gadri') and NU (aka content clause particles, or abstractors) respectively. They always go at the end.\n\nSentence 3: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: If “ku se ka vau kei” were a valid  sumti, or absent from the sentence, then #3 would be correct. -- Annotator 2 comment: {tunba} is the selbri (predicate) and {le ka stela vau kei se steba} is the sumti (argument).\n\nSentence 4: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: This does not make any sense. “se ka vau” does not exist in Lojban, but even if it did, the individual words that make it up have nothing to do with causality. -- Annotator 2 comment: {se} switches the places of slots in a predicate. {ka} is used to create a content clause about properties. {vau} is a terminator for bridi (assertions). To talk about causes, {se ja'e} should be used. Example: {se ja'e lo su'u stela} = \"because of the key\"\n\nSentence 5: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: False. (B) follows the basic pattern “[sumti] [selbri]”.“le ka stela vau kei se steba ku” is the sumti (argument), and “tunba” is the selbri (predicate). -- Annotator 2 comment: _see following comments_\n\nSentence 6: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: 1. “le ka” is a very common construction. 2. “ku tunba” is not a unit. The “ku” belongs to the sumti (argument) headed by “le”. In other words, the LLM is grouping the words incorrectly into sentence constituents. -- Annotator 2 comment: LE + NU is a very common construction in Lojban. All NU convert the contents within it into a selbri, which can then be converted into a sumti by LE.  H