{"﻿original_id": "stim146_gpt4_B_1", "validator_id": "210", "prompt": "The topic at issue is whether Sentence 1 of a proposed solution to a problem is correct or flawed. A sentence is 'flawed' if it contains one or more claims that do not make logical sense, are untrue, seem true but misleading, or have other serious issues. Here is the problem and proposed solution:\n\nProblem:\nLojban is a constructed human language created by the Logical Language Group in 1987. Which of the following is written in valid, grammatical standard Lojban?\n\n(A) lo ve snada cu go'a\n(B) ve snada cu lo go'a\n\nProposed solution:\n1. In order to understand why (B) ve snada cu lo go'a is the better answer, let's break down the components of each sentence.\n2. (A) lo ve snada cu go'a (B) ve snada cu lo go'a In Lojban, \"lo\" is a determiner that means \"the thing(s) that really is(are)\"; it is used to specify a particular object or objects.\n3. \"ve snada\" means \"to succeed\" or \"to achieve\", and \"cu\" is a separator that is used to separate the subject from the predicate.\n4. Finally, \"go'a\" is a pro-bridi that refers to the previous bridi (a bridi is a basic unit of meaning in Lojban, similar to a clause in English).\n5. Now, let's analyze the structure of each sentence.\n6. (A) lo ve snada cu go'a This sentence can be translated as \"The thing(s) that really is(are) succeeding is(are) the same as the previous bridi.\"\n7. However, this sentence is not grammatically correct because \"lo\" should be followed by a selbri (a predicate), not a sumti (an argument) like \"ve snada\".\n8. This makes the sentence difficult to understand and parse.\n9. (B) ve snada cu lo go'a This sentence can be translated as \"To succeed is the same as the thing(s) that really is(are) the previous bridi.\"\n10. In this sentence, \"ve snada\" is correctly used as a selbri, and \"lo go'a\" is correctly used as a sumti.\n11. This sentence is grammatically correct and conveys a clear meaning.\n12. Therefore, the better answer is (B) ve snada cu lo go'a, as it is grammatically correct and conveys a clear meaning, while (A) lo ve snada cu go'a is not grammatically correct and is difficult to understand.\n\nIs Sentence 1 of the proposed solution correct or flawed?", "sentence": "1", "choice_a": "Sentence 1 is correct.", "choice_b": "Sentence 1 is flawed.", "correct_answer": "FLAWED", "original_key": "B", "explanation": "Sentence 1: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: Implies that (B) is better, which is false. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see succeeding comments_\n\nSentence 2: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 3: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “snada” means “to succeed”, but “ve snada” is undefined.  “ve” swaps the 1st and 4th argument places of the following predicate, but “snada” only has three defined argument places. Using “ve” to swap the 1st argument place with a non-existent 4th argument place is semantically undefined. -- Annotator 2 comment: {ve snada} is untranslatable because it has an out of bounds error.\n\nSentence 4: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “go’a” refers to a recent bridi, not the previous bridi. -- Annotator 2 comment: {go'a} refers to a recent bridi. For the most recent bridi, {go'i} is required.\n\nSentence 5: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 6: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: The comment for #2 applies here. too.  The translation of “go’a” is slightly off as well (although it is consistent with what #4 says).  Assuming “lo ve snada” could be translated as “the things that are succeeding”, (A) could be translated as “The things that are succeeding (also) satisfy the  predicate expressed by a recent (salient) bridi.” -- Annotator 2 comment: (A) is untranslatable because it has an out of bounds error.\n\nSentence 7: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “lo” should be followed by a selbri (a predicate), yes. But “ve snada” IS a predicate (albeit a c