{"﻿original_id": "stim172_gpt3.5_A_1", "validator_id": "353", "prompt": "The topic at issue is whether Sentence 1 of a proposed solution to a problem is correct or flawed. A sentence is 'flawed' if it contains one or more claims that do not make logical sense, are untrue, seem true but misleading, or have other serious issues. Here is the problem and proposed solution:\n\nProblem:\nLojban is a constructed human language created by the Logical Language Group in 1987. Which of the following is written in valid, grammatical standard Lojban?\n\n(A) se .i cu dapma racli le\n(B) .i le se dapma cu racli\n\nProposed solution:\n1. The correct answer is (A) se .i cu dapma racli le.\n2. This is because in Lojban, the verb comes before the object it acts upon.\n3. In (B), the verb \"dapma\" comes after the object \"se\", which is incorrect grammar.\n4. Additionally, the use of \"le\" in (A) indicates that the speaker is referring to a specific thing, while (B) does not specify which thing is being referred to.\n5. Therefore, (A) is the correct and grammatical answer.\n\nIs Sentence 1 of the proposed solution correct or flawed?", "sentence": "1", "choice_a": "Sentence 1 is flawed.", "choice_b": "Sentence 1 is correct.", "correct_answer": "FLAWED", "original_key": "A", "explanation": "Sentence 1: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: (A) contains three syntax errors. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see succeeding comments_\n\nSentence 2: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: Lojban has free word order, so the object can appear before or after the verb. -- Annotator 2 comment: The canonical word order in Lojban is SVO. However, Lojban has free word order so long as the components are properly marked.\n\nSentence 3: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: The verb in sentence (B) is not “dapma” but “racli”.  (B) does not have an object; it only has a subject.  “se” is not the object. It is not even a sumti (argument). -- Annotator 2 comment: {se} is not an object, but a particle.\n\nSentence 4: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: Yes, the determiner “le” makes a specific reference, specifically to something which satisfies the predicate following “le”.  In (A), “le” is placed at the end of the sentence and lacks a predicate complement, causing a parse fail.  Next, #4 claims that, in (B), what  is being referred to is not specified. But the exact opposite is the case: “le” is followed by the predicate “se dapma” (“to be cursed”), thus forming an expression that refers to a specific cursed thing or person. So it is not (B) but (A) that doesn’t specifiy which thing is being referred to. -- Annotator 2 comment: {le} does mean that the speaker has an intended specific referent. However, it has nothing to do with the validity of the sentences.\n\nSentence 5: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: Absolute hogwash. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see previous comments_\n\n"}
