g.  The claim that “le” is used as a predicate marker in (A) is confusing. “Predicate marker” is normally how the particle “cu” is described (see also previous comment). There is no reason to think that “le” is doing anything of that sort in (A). Rather, “le” is simply dangling at the end of the sentence, which is another syntax error. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 5: AMBIGUOUS -- Annotator 1 comment: Flawed reasoning (#4) which fails to address the main issues of (A) precisely. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\n"}
{"﻿original_id": "stim172_gpt4_A_1", "validator_id": "360", "prompt": "The topic at issue is whether Sentence 1 of a proposed solution to a problem is correct or flawed. A sentence is 'flawed' if it contains one or more claims that do not make logical sense, are untrue, seem true but misleading, or have other serious issues. Here is the problem and proposed solution:\n\nProblem:\nLojban is a constructed human language created by the Logical Language Group in 1987. Which of the following is written in valid, grammatical standard Lojban?\n\n(A) se .i cu dapma racli le\n(B) .i le se dapma cu racli\n\nProposed solution:\n1. In order to understand why (A) is the correct answer, let's break down the two options and analyze their grammatical structure.\n2. Option (A) reads: \"se .i cu dapma racli le\" Option (B) reads: \".i le se dapma cu racli\" First, let's look at option (B).\n3. The sentence starts with \".i\", which is a simple sentence separator in Lojban.\n4. It is typically used to separate two sentences, but in this case, there is no preceding sentence, which makes the use of \".i\" at the beginning of the sentence unusual and potentially incorrect.\n5. Now, let's examine option (A).\n6. The sentence starts with \"se\", which is a conversion operator in Lojban.\n7. It is used to change the order of the arguments in a predicate.\n8. In this case, it is followed by \".i\", which, as mentioned earlier, is a sentence separator.\n9. However, in this context, it is used correctly, as it separates the conversion operator \"se\" from the rest of the sentence.\n10. Next, we have \"cu\", which is a separator used to prevent ambiguity in Lojban sentences.\n11. It is followed by \"dapma\", which is a verb meaning \"to curse\" or \"to damn\".\n12. Then, we have \"racli\", which means \"rational\" or \"reasonable\", and finally, \"le\", which is a definite article meaning \"the\".\n13. Putting it all together, option (A) can be translated as \"The reasonable curse is reversed.\"\n14. This sentence is grammatically correct and makes logical sense in Lojban.\n15. In conclusion, option (A) \"se .i cu dapma racli le\" is the better answer, as it is a valid, grammatical standard Lojban sentence.\n16. Option (B) \".i le se dapma cu racli\" is incorrect due to the misplaced sentence separator \".i\" at the beginning of the sentence.\n\nIs Sentence 1 of the proposed solution correct or flawed?", "sentence": "1", "choice_a": "Sentence 1 is correct.", "choice_b": "Sentence 1 is flawed.", "correct_answer": "FLAWED", "original_key": "B", "explanation": "Sentence 1: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: (A) is not the correct answer. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see succeeding comments_\n\nSentence 2: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 3: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 4: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: It is not unusual for Lojban speakers to start their speech with “.i” out of habit even when there is no preceding sentence from which to separate the next one.  This use of “.i” is entirely correct. -- Annotator 2 comment: It is licit to use {.i} regardless of whether or not there is a preceding sentence.\n\nSentence 5: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 6: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 7: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 8: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 9: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: No, “se” must not be separated from the predicate whose argument order it is to change. -- Annotator 2 comment: This is incorrect usage: {se} must come immediately before the predicate it applies to.\n\nSentence 10: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 11: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 12: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 13: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: It is easy to see how it arrived at this translation, but it is wrong on multiple levels.   “reversed” from “se”, which “reverses” the order of a predicate’s 1st and 2nd argument. “se” does not MEAN “reverse”, it PERFORMS a reverse.  Also, “se” is not even part of the same sentence, as there is an “.i” separating “se” from the sentence.  - The only interpretable bits of (A) that remain after removing all the misplaced function words is the compound predicate “dapma racli” (“to be rational in a way related to a curse”). In (A) this compound is translated backwards. -- Annotator 2 comment: (A) cannot be translated accurately as it is ungrammatical. \"The reasonable curse is reversed.\" I might translate as {.i gafygau fi le racli te dapma lo dukti be ri}.\n\nSentence 14: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: I’m assuming that “this sentence” in #14 refers to the English translation in #13, which is indeed a grammatically correct English sentence.  This is no basis for judging whether the Lojban sentence itself makes “logical sense” (it does not). -- Annotator 2 comment: _see previous comments_\n\nSentence 15: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: (A) is not even remotely grammatically correct. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see previous comments_\n\nSentence 16: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “.i” is not misplaced in (B) (see comment on #4).  It is striking how “.i” is the only word the model analyzed about (B) and then followed it up with a much lengthier argument in favor of (A), where it actually looked at the individual parts of the sentence. Unfortunately, that wasn’t enough to realize that (A) is full of mistakes, mainly because it performed a lexical analysis rather than a syntactic one. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see 9's comment_\n\n"}
