wer.\n\nIs Sentence 1 of the proposed solution correct or flawed?", "sentence": "1", "choice_a": "Sentence 1 is correct.", "choice_b": "Sentence 1 is flawed.", "correct_answer": "CORRECT", "original_key": "A", "explanation": "Sentence 1: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 2: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: (A) is not better than (B). -- Annotator 2 comment: _see succeeding comments_\n\nSentence 3: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 4: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: - “le ku srana” does not parse.  “the thing which is related” is what “le srana ku” would mean, which has “ku” in the right place.  - “cu” can be described as a  separator (it marks the predicate, thereby preventing the predicate from forming unwanted compounds with any preceding arguments), but it is misleading to state that it “helps to clarify” anything. “cu” is either required in a sentence or not. When it is required, removing it does not make the structure less clear; instead, it CHANGES the structure, resulting in a different parse tree unambiguously. -- Annotator 2 comment: {le ku srana} does not mean \"the thing that is related\"; that would be {le srana ku}.\n\nSentence 5: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: The right-most component of a compound determines the base meaning of a compound.  “lo cerda drani” contains “cerda” (heir) and “drani” (correct), so “lo cerda drani” refers to something that is correct which has something to do with heirs.   “correct heir” is therefore not a good translation.  Better would be “something which is right to inherit”. -- Annotator 2 comment: {lo cerda drani} means \"the heirly-correct thing\". \"The correct heir\" would be {lo drani cerda}.\n\nSentence 6: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: Since (A) is grammatically broken, a proper translation is impossible.  See the previos comment for why “correct heir” is not a good translation. -- Annotator 2 comment: (A) is untranslatable because it is ungrammatical.\n\nSentence 7: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 8: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: For some reason, #8 treats “le drani cu cerda” as separate from the first argument of the sentence “lo srana ku”, thinking that “le drani” is the first argument of “cerda” when it is really the second argument.  [lo srana ku] [le drani] cu [cerda]  [the related thing] [the correct one] does [stand to inherit] -- Annotator 2 comment: {le drani cu cerda} does indeed mean \"the correct one is the heir\", when considered on its own. However, in this case, {le drani} occupies the x1 parameter of cerda (the heir). In the original sentence, {lo srana ku} occupies the x1 and {le drani} occupies the x2 (what x1 is heir to). This is misleading.\n\nSentence 9: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “ku” is used correctly. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see succeeding comments_\n\nSentence 10: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “ku” closes the structure headed by “lo”, which is “lo srana”.   It does not “disrupt”. It is simply where it belongs.  Words like “disrupt” read very strange in descriptions of Lojban syntax, which should always allow a very clear cut judgement. -- Annotator 2 comment: {ku} is indeed the terminator for {le} and {lo}. However, the way it is used, placed after {srana}, is correct.\n\nSentence 11: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: (A) is not better than (B). It contains two red flags (“lo ku” and “cu lo”) which should immediately lead to (A)’s rejection. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see previous comments_\n\nSentence 12: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “ku” is used correctly in (B). -- Annotator 2 comment: _See 10's comment_\n\nSentence 13: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: (A) is not correct. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see previous comments_\n\n"}
