 is untranslatable because it is ungrammatical.\n\nSentence 7: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 8: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: For some reason, #8 treats “le drani cu cerda” as separate from the first argument of the sentence “lo srana ku”, thinking that “le drani” is the first argument of “cerda” when it is really the second argument.  [lo srana ku] [le drani] cu [cerda]  [the related thing] [the correct one] does [stand to inherit] -- Annotator 2 comment: {le drani cu cerda} does indeed mean \"the correct one is the heir\", when considered on its own. However, in this case, {le drani} occupies the x1 parameter of cerda (the heir). In the original sentence, {lo srana ku} occupies the x1 and {le drani} occupies the x2 (what x1 is heir to). This is misleading.\n\nSentence 9: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “ku” is used correctly. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see succeeding comments_\n\nSentence 10: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “ku” closes the structure headed by “lo”, which is “lo srana”.   It does not “disrupt”. It is simply where it belongs.  Words like “disrupt” read very strange in descriptions of Lojban syntax, which should always allow a very clear cut judgement. -- Annotator 2 comment: {ku} is indeed the terminator for {le} and {lo}. However, the way it is used, placed after {srana}, is correct.\n\nSentence 11: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: (A) is not better than (B). It contains two red flags (“lo ku” and “cu lo”) which should immediately lead to (A)’s rejection. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see previous comments_\n\nSentence 12: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “ku” is used correctly in (B). -- Annotator 2 comment: _See 10's comment_\n\nSentence 13: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: (A) is not correct. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see previous comments_\n\n"}
