{"﻿original_id": "stim178_gpt3.5_B_2", "validator_id": "457", "prompt": "The topic at issue is whether Sentence 2 of a proposed solution to a problem is correct or flawed. A sentence is 'flawed' if it contains one or more claims that do not make logical sense, are untrue, seem true but misleading, or have other serious issues. Here is the problem and proposed solution:\n\nProblem:\nLojban is a constructed human language created by the Logical Language Group in 1987. Which of the following is written in valid, grammatical standard Lojban?\n\n(A) jelca le ko'u lakne cumla bruna li'u jatna cu rinka vi le lu\n(B) le vi rinka cu bruna cumla lu lakne ko'u le jelca jatna li'u\n\nProposed solution:\n1. The correct answer is (B) because it follows the proper Lojban sentence structure.\n2. In Lojban, the verb comes first, followed by the subject, and then the object.\n3. In (B), the verb \"rinka\" comes first, followed by the subject \"le vi rinka\" (which means \"the cause of this\") and then the object \"lu lakne ko'u le jelca jatna li'u\" (which means \"the flame of my wax brother\").\n4. In (A), the sentence structure is incorrect.\n5. The verb \"rinka\" comes last, which is not proper Lojban sentence structure.\n6. Additionally, the subject \"jatna\" (which means \"driver\") is not properly connected to the rest of the sentence.\n7. Therefore, (B) is the correct answer and (A) is incorrect.\n\nIs Sentence 2 of the proposed solution correct or flawed?", "sentence": "2", "choice_a": "Sentence 2 is flawed.", "choice_b": "Sentence 2 is correct.", "correct_answer": "FLAWED", "original_key": "A", "explanation": "Sentence 1: AMBIGUOUS -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: Both are incorrect. _see previous comments_\n\nSentence 2: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: Lojban has free word order. VSO is just one of many ways a sentence can be structured. -- Annotator 2 comment: The canonical word order in Lojban is SVO. However, Lojban has free word order so long as the components are properly marked.\n\nSentence 3: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: This is so bad.  - “rinka” does not come first, it’s “le vi rinka” that comes first. - It makes absolutely no sense to say that “rinka” is followed by “le vi rinka”; there is only a single occurrence of “rinka” in the sentence. - “le vi rinka” means “this nearby event which causes another event”, not “the cause of this nearby thing”. - “lakne” means “likely”, “lakse” means “wax”.  - “jatna” (“boss”) wasn’t translated at all - “jelca” means “to burn”, not “flame”.  #3 says (B) follows VSO word order, but it actually has SVO word order. -- Annotator 2 comment: {rinka} as a fragment considered on its own is indeed a verb, but in the context of the sentence, it is inside a LE-construct (noun phrase), turning it into an argument.   {le vi rinka} means \"the cause here\". \"The cause of this\" is {le rinka be vi}. {lu lakne ko'u le jelca jatna li'u} is an invalid sentence, because {le jelca jatna} occupies the parameter x3 of {lakne}, but {lakne} does not have an x3. \"The flame of my wax brother\" is {le fagri pe le lakse bruna be mi}.\n\nSentence 4: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 5: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: The verb is allowed to come last in a sentence. -- Annotator 2 comment: The canonical word order in Lojban is SVO. However, Lojban has free word order so long as the components are properly marked.\n\nSentence 6: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: If it is the subject, how is it not connected?  It’s actually hard to find anything in (A) that could be considered the subject. The sentence is that broken.  Also, “jatna” means “boss/leader/captain”, not “driver”. -- Annotator 2 comment: {jatna} means \"is a commander\". \"Driver\" is {lo sazri}.\n\nSentence 7: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: Extremely flawed reasoning. -- Annotator 2 comment: Both are incorrect. _see previous comments_.\n\n"}
