{"﻿original_id": "stim184_gpt3.5_B_1", "validator_id": "521", "prompt": "The topic at issue is whether Sentence 1 of a proposed solution to a problem is correct or flawed. A sentence is 'flawed' if it contains one or more claims that do not make logical sense, are untrue, seem true but misleading, or have other serious issues. Here is the problem and proposed solution:\n\nProblem:\nLojban is a constructed human language created by the Logical Language Group in 1987. Which of the following is written in valid, grammatical standard Lojban?\n\n(A) mi'o cu traji jursa la loras.\n(B) traji la cu loras. jursa mi'o\n\nProposed solution:\n1. The correct answer is (B) \"traji la cu loras. jursa mi'o\".\n2. This is because in Lojban, the predicate comes before the subject.\n3. So, \"traji\" (meaning \"most\") should come before \"la cu loras\" (meaning \"Laura\").\n4. Additionally, \"mi'o\" (meaning \"we\") should come after \"jursa\" (meaning \"fierce\") to indicate that \"we\" are the fierce ones.\n5. As for (A) \"mi'o cu traji jursa la loras\", this is not grammatically correct because the predicate \"traji\" should come before the subject \"la loras\".\n6. Additionally, \"mi'o\" should come after \"jursa\" to indicate that \"we\" are the fierce ones.\n7. Therefore, (B) is the correct answer and (A) is incorrect.\n\nIs Sentence 1 of the proposed solution correct or flawed?", "sentence": "1", "choice_a": "Sentence 1 is flawed.", "choice_b": "Sentence 1 is correct.", "correct_answer": "FLAWED", "original_key": "A", "explanation": "Sentence 1: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: (B) is not correct. It contains two predicates (there can only be one per clause) and the “cu” is misplaced. -- Annotator 2 comment: Both are incorrect. _see succeeding comments_\n\nSentence 2: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: Lojban has free word order. The predicate can come before, after, or between its arguments. -- Annotator 2 comment: The canonical word order in Lojban is SVO. However, Lojban has free word order so long as the components are properly marked.\n\nSentence 3: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “la cu loras” is missing the period as part of the name word. Also, the “cu” does not belong there, which #3 doesn’t acknowledge. (Without “cu”, “Laura” is correct) -- Annotator 2 comment: {traji} means \"most\" specifically in the superlative sense only (e.g. \"most beautiful\"). {la loras} I would translate as \"Lora\". \"Laura\" I would transliterate as {la lauras}.\n\nSentence 4: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: #4 seems to treat “jursa mi’o” as a separate sentence or claim from “traji la cu loras.”, which is understandable given the fact that a sentence cannot have more than one predicate. However, sentence breaks must be marked with “.i”. As there is no “.i” in (B), the language model should have seen the two predicates and rejected the sentence. The other problem with #4 is that it’s following the incorrect assumption made in #2 that the predicate must precede the subject.  In actual Lojban, when the subject follows the predicate, the subject must carry the subject marker “fa” or it will fall into the second argument place of the prdicate. -- Annotator 2 comment: {jursa} can only accept event arguments in its parameter x1. Objects and entities cannot be {jursa}, only events can.\n\nSentence 5: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: - I’m guessing by “should come before” it means “immediately before”, otherwise #5 makes no sense, given that “traji” does precede “la loras” in the string of symbols that is (A). In any case, it is not true that the predicate must come before the subject.  - #5 says “traji” is the predicate. But the predicate is the compound “traji jursa”.  - “la loras.” is not the subject. The subject is “mi’o”, because it precedes the predicate. For “la loras.” to be the subject, it would have to be marked with “fa”: “fa la noras.”  - “loras” is missing the period. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see 2's comment_\n\nSentence 6: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: #