{"﻿original_id": "stim168_gpt3.5_A_1", "validator_id": "573", "prompt": "The topic at issue is whether Sentence 1 of a proposed solution to a problem is correct or flawed. A sentence is 'flawed' if it contains one or more claims that do not make logical sense, are untrue, seem true but misleading, or have other serious issues. Here is the problem and proposed solution:\n\nProblem:\nLojban is a constructed human language created by the Logical Language Group in 1987. Which of the following is written in valid, grammatical standard Lojban?\n\n(A) la djim. pu xance la dikca loras.\n(B) pu la loras. xance dikca la djim.\n\nProposed solution:\n1. The correct answer is (A) because it follows the proper Lojban sentence structure.\n2. In Lojban, the subject comes first, followed by the tense, and then the predicate.\n3. In (A), \"la djim\" is the subject, \"pu\" is the tense indicating past tense, and \"xance la dikca loras\" is the predicate.\n4. This follows the correct sentence structure.\n5. In (B), the sentence structure is incorrect.\n6. \"pu\" comes before the subject \"la loras\", which is not proper Lojban sentence structure.\n7. Additionally, \"dikca\" and \"xance\" are switched in the predicate, which also makes the sentence grammatically incorrect.\n8. Therefore, (A) is the correct answer and (B) is incorrect.\n\nIs Sentence 1 of the proposed solution correct or flawed?", "sentence": "1", "choice_a": "Sentence 1 is flawed.", "choice_b": "Sentence 1 is correct.", "correct_answer": "FLAWED", "original_key": "A", "explanation": "Sentence 1: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: (A) is ungrammatical under standard rules, due to the  dangling name word (“loras.”) at the end of the sentence. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see succeeding comments_\n\nSentence 2: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: This is not generally true.  Lojban has free word order.  The predicate can come before, between, or after, its arguments.    Tense need not be overtly stated, and it can be expressed in many different positions. -- Annotator 2 comment: The canonical word order in Lojban is SVO. However, Lojban has free word order so long as the components are properly marked.\n\nSentence 3: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: The predicate is “xance”, and “la dikca” is the object, or the second argument.  “loras.” is stranded by itself, causing a parsefail. Name words (words that end in a consonant), must be preceded by the name article “la” to be able to function as an argument.   There is another issue with #3: the name words “djim.” and “loras.” should not lose the period after their name. In Lojban, the period is not punctuation but a consonant, pronounced as a glottal stop or a pause. -- Annotator 2 comment: {xance la dikca loras} is a bridi tail. {xance} is the predicate.\n\nSentence 4: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: (A) is neither correct, nor does it follow the sentence structure described in #2. (see previous comment) -- Annotator 2 comment: _see 2's comment_\n\nSentence 5: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: (B) is correct. -- Annotator 2 comment: (B) is correct. _see succeeding comments_\n\nSentence 6: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: In (B), “la loras.” is not the subject. The tense marker “pu” can function as a preposition, in which case it means “(temporally) before”.  “pu la loras.” therefore means “before Laura”.   Normally, “pu” would be followed by an argument that refers to an event. Maybe someone is referring to a specific event named “Laura”. That would avoid a type mismatch.  The name word “loras.” should not lose the period after the name. The period is not punctuation but a consonant, pronounced as a glottal stop or a pause. -- Annotator 2 comment: The structure {pu X} where X is an event argument can occur anywhere in the sentence, so long as it is not enclosed in any clauses (otherwise, the {pu X} structure will apply to the clause rather than the main sentence).\n\nSentence 7: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: I don’t understand what “switched in the predicate” means.  “xance dikca” is a compound predicate that means something like “hand-related electricity”, which could refer to electric current in someone’s hand. -- Annotator 2 comment: The order of predicates in a verb phrase does not matter _grammatically_ speaking – though it has a big effect on the semantics.\n\nSentence 8: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: No, (A) is wrong and (B) is right. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see previous comments_\n\n"}
