er.\n\nIs Sentence 11 of the proposed solution correct or flawed?", "sentence": "11", "choice_a": "Sentence 11 is flawed.", "choice_b": "Sentence 11 is correct.", "correct_answer": "CORRECT", "original_key": "B", "explanation": "Sentence 1: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 2: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 3: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 4: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: This is the only inaccurate bit in #4.   The language model is right in taking issue with the “loras.” at the end of the sentence, but the reason that’s given is not quite correct.  “loras.” is a valid Lojban word. It is a name word, which is a word that ends in a consonant. Such words can only function as names, but to participate in a sentence, they need to be preceded by the name article “la”. This article is missing in (A), which causes “loras.” to be out of place. -- Annotator 2 comment: {loras is a morphologically valid Lojban name word.\n\nSentence 5: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 6: AMBIGUOUS -- Annotator 1 comment: [No comment] -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 7: AMBIGUOUS -- Annotator 1 comment: This definition is correct, but in (B), “pu” acts as a preposition on “la loras.”, which changes the entire structure of the sentence. As #8 further reveals, this was missed. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 8: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “In the past” is incorrect because “pu” acts as a preposition in (B). “pu la loras.” means “(temporally) before Laura”  The rest of the translation is also incorrect. A proper translation would be:  “Before Laura, there was hand-related electricity in Jim.” -- Annotator 2 comment: This is an incorrect translation. I would translate (B) as \"After Loras (\"loras\" being the name of an event),