{"﻿original_id": "stim161_gpt3.5_A_2", "validator_id": "724", "prompt": "The topic at issue is whether Sentence 2 of a proposed solution to a problem is correct or flawed. A sentence is 'flawed' if it contains one or more claims that do not make logical sense, are untrue, seem true but misleading, or have other serious issues. Here is the problem and proposed solution:\n\nProblem:\nLojban is a constructed human language created by the Logical Language Group in 1987. Which of the following is written in valid, grammatical standard Lojban?\n\n(A) pe'u. djan. do'u te sodva le kukte\n(B) kukte pe'u. le djan. sodva do'u te\n\nProposed solution:\n1. The correct answer is (A) pe'u. djan. do'u te sodva le kukte.\n2. This is because in Lojban, the verb comes first in a sentence, followed by the subject and then the object.\n3. In (A), the verb \"sodva\" (to taste) comes first, followed by the subject \"djan\" (John) and then the object \"le kukte\" (the cake).\n4. In (B), the word order is incorrect, with the object \"kukte\" coming first, followed by the subject \"le djan\" (the John) and then the verb \"sodva\".\n5. This is not a valid sentence structure in Lojban and therefore (B) is incorrect.\n\nIs Sentence 2 of the proposed solution correct or flawed?", "sentence": "2", "choice_a": "Sentence 2 is correct.", "choice_b": "Sentence 2 is flawed.", "correct_answer": "FLAWED", "original_key": "B", "explanation": "Sentence 1: AMBIGUOUS -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: Both are incorrect. _see succeeding comments_\n\nSentence 2: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: Lojban has free word order. The predicate can come before, between, or after, its arguments. -- Annotator 2 comment: The canonical word order in Lojban is SVO. However, Lojban has free word order so long as the components are properly marked.\n\nSentence 3: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: Lots of mistakes here: - “sodva” means “soda, carbonated beverage”, not “taste”. - “kukte” means “delicious”, not “cake” - #3 says “sodva” precedes “djan”, but looking at (A), one can see that “djan” comes first in the string. - “djan.” is not the subject. It is the complement of the vocative “pe’u”, which means “please”, and the request is directed at someone named John. -- Annotator 2 comment: In (A), {djan} comes before {sodva}.  {sodva} means \"is soda\". \"To taste\" is {vrusyga'e}. {le kukte} means \"the delicious thing\". \"The cake\" is {le titnanba}.\n\nSentence 4: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “kukte” cannot be said to be the object. Predicate words cannot function as arguments without being preceded by a determiner such as “le”.  The sentence is too jumbled to unambiguously identify its constituents, so the rest of #4 is neither right nor wrong. However, “le djan” is not possible under traditional rules, as name words like “djan” can only take the name determiner “la”, which is something the analysis should have picked up on. -- Annotator 2 comment: The word order is incorrect, but it has nothing to do with traditional SVO word ordering. Rather, {djan} is being nominalized by {le}, but {djan} is a name word, and not a native Lojban word. Therefore, it must be nominalized by {la} (or not have a nominalizer at all, in this case, as {pe'u} is a vocative).   Also, {te} operates on predicates and must have a predicate immediately after it, but there is no such predicate in (B).\n\nSentence 5: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: #4 failed to identify the actual problems with (B), instead trying to match (B) against #2, which is a flawed premise. Both (A) and (B) were analyzed incorrectly and unconvincingly, so the conclusion in #5 holds no weight. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see 4's comment_\n\n"}
