{"﻿original_id": "stim161_gpt3.5_B_1", "validator_id": "728", "prompt": "The topic at issue is whether Sentence 1 of a proposed solution to a problem is correct or flawed. A sentence is 'flawed' if it contains one or more claims that do not make logical sense, are untrue, seem true but misleading, or have other serious issues. Here is the problem and proposed solution:\n\nProblem:\nLojban is a constructed human language created by the Logical Language Group in 1987. Which of the following is written in valid, grammatical standard Lojban?\n\n(A) pe'u. djan. do'u te sodva le kukte\n(B) kukte pe'u. le djan. sodva do'u te\n\nProposed solution:\n1. The correct answer is (B) because it follows the proper Lojban sentence structure.\n2. In Lojban, the verb always comes first, followed by the subject and then the object.\n3. In (A), \"pe'u\" is not a verb and therefore cannot come first.\n4. In (B), \"kukte\" is the verb and comes first, followed by the subject \"le djan.\" and then the object \"do'u te sodva.\"\n5. This sentence structure is grammatically correct in Lojban and therefore (B) is the correct answer.\n\nIs Sentence 1 of the proposed solution correct or flawed?", "sentence": "1", "choice_a": "Sentence 1 is correct.", "choice_b": "Sentence 1 is flawed.", "correct_answer": "FLAWED", "original_key": "B", "explanation": "Sentence 1: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: (B) is not correct as it violates several grammar rules. -- Annotator 2 comment: Both are incorrect. _see succeeding answers_\n\nSentence 2: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: Lojban has free word order. The predicate can come before, between, or after, its arguments. -- Annotator 2 comment: The canonical word order in Lojban is SVO. However, Lojban has free word order so long as the components are properly marked.\n\nSentence 3: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “pe’u” is a vocative meaning “please”. Vocative phrases are free modifiers, which can be placed freely almost anywhere in a sentence without affecting the rest of the sentence. It can come first. -- Annotator 2 comment: {pe'u} is indeed not a verb. However, it is a vocative (member of part of speech COI) and so has nearly free word order. Placing it at the start of the sentence is licit.\n\nSentence 4: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “le djan.” cannot be the subject, for two reasons: - it follows “pe’u”, which makes it an argument of “pe’u” rather than an argument of the verb “kukte”. - for an argument following the verb to function as the subject, it must carry the subject marker “fa”  Also, “le djan” is not possible under traditional rules, as name words like “djan” can only take the name determiner “la”, and not “le”.  do’u te sodva” does not even remotely have the shape of something that could be an object or argument of any kind.  “do’u” is the terminator for “pe’u”. It does not form a unit with “te sodva”. “te sodva” is a predicate and predicates need a determiner (like “le”) to be able to function as arguments. -- Annotator 2 comment: {le djan} is an invalid argument because {djan} is a name word and not a native Lojban word. Therefore, it must be nominalized by {la}, not {le}. (It is also permitted in this case not to have any nominalizer at all, because {pe'u} is a vocative.) Further, it is being taken up by {pe'u}, which accepts a single argument – it is not being passed into any of the parameters of the main predicate. In (B), the sequence is {sodva do'u te}, not {do'u te sodva}.\n\nSentence 5: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: (B) is very far from grammatically correct. -- Annotator 2 comment: Both are incorrect. _see previous answers_\n\n"}
