ption (B) is incorrect due to its grammatical errors and unclear meaning.\n\nIs Sentence 2 of the proposed solution correct or flawed?", "sentence": "2", "choice_a": "Sentence 2 is flawed.", "choice_b": "Sentence 2 is correct.", "correct_answer": "CORRECT", "original_key": "B", "explanation": "Sentence 1: AMBIGUOUS -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: Both are incorrect. _see succeeding comments_\n\nSentence 2: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 3: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: #3 is a good breakdown of (A), but it does contain one mistake.  “te sodva” does not mean “to serve a drink”. “sodva” is a predicate meaning “to be a carbonated beverage”, and it has two argument places: the beverage, and the flavor/brand. “te” is a predicate modifier which swaps the first and third(!) argument place. Since “sodva” only has two argument places, it has the effect of swapping an undefined place with the first argument place, which leads to undefined behavior.  “Please, John, [something about a carbonated beverage of the delicious brand]” -- Annotator 2 comment: {sodva} means \"is soda\". \"To serve a drink\" is {dunda lo djalitki}.  The sentence is untranslatable because it has an out of bounds error.\n\nSentence 4: AMBIGUOUS -- Annotator 1 comment: This is okay, but it is misleading to call “kukte” an adjective and “sodva” a noun. Lojban does not distinguish adjectives, nouns and verbs. There are only predicates (calling everything a verb would also be acceptable). -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 5: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 6: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: see ccomment on #3 -- Annotator 2 comment: Both are incorrect. _see previous comments_\n\nSentence 7: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\n"}
