nalizer at all, because {pe'u} is a vocative.)  {sodva} here is not being nominalized or put into other constructs, so it is a verb, not a noun. This is ungrammatical, as each sentence can only have one main verb.  {do'u te} is just two particles put together – not a verb phrase and is nonsensical.\n\nSentence 4: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “pe’u” can be placed almost anywhere, including the beginning of the sentence. -- Annotator 2 comment: {pe'u} is a vocative (member of part of speech COI) and so has nearly free word order. Placing it at the start of the sentence is licit.\n\nSentence 5: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “pe’u” always modifies the entire sentence, no matter where it is placed.  That said, it’s not clear how “pe’u”’s position in (B) would modify the verb “to serve”, which the language model thinks is the word “te” (see #3). But that word is not adjacent to “pe’u”. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see 3's comment_. Further, {pe'u} stands on its own and is not a verb operator. It cannot modify verbs.\n\nSentence 6: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “djan” is the complement of “pe’u”, and “do’u” terminates the “pe’u” phrase, which is entirely correct.  The claim that “do’u” separates “djan” from “le kukte” is strange… Even if “do’u” were removed, there would still be the predicate “te sodva” separating “djan” from “le kukte”. But it’s not clear why this separation would be an issue, anyway. Very confusing. -- Annotator 2 comment: It is correct to separate {djan} from {le kukte}.\n\nSentence 7: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “sodva” is a predicate, not a noun. “le djan.” does not form a noun phrase with “sodva”. -- Annotator 2 comment: {djan} is being nominalized by {le}, but {djan} is a name word, and not a native Lojban word. Therefore, it must be nominalized by {la}. Further, it is illicit to have both name words and native Lojban words in the same noun phrase.\n\nSentence 8: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: Ouch.  “te sodva”, while semantically odd (see comment on #2), is at least very obviously shaped like a predicate. “do’u te” is a vocative terminator followed by a predicate modifier. It is as far from a “verb phrase” as it gets. -- Annotator 2 comment: {do'u te} is just two particles put together and does not constitute a verb phrase – neither of the two words is a verb.\n\nSentence 9: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “te” still does not mean “to serve” (see comment on #2).  “te” is also not connected to anything else in sentence (B). It is dangling at the end of the sentence.  There is nothing in (B) that means “the tasty John’s soda”. -- Annotator 2 comment: None of the verbs in (B) mean \"to serve\".\n\nSentence 10: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: It has none of the things mentioned. -- Annotator 2 comment: Both are incorrect. _see previous comments_\n\nSentence 11: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: (A) contains no relevant grammatical errors. -- Annotator 2 comment: (A)'s issue is the out of bounds error triggered by {te}, not syntax.\n\n"}
