{"﻿original_id": "stim186_gpt4_A_1", "validator_id": "943", "prompt": "The topic at issue is whether Sentence 1 of a proposed solution to a problem is correct or flawed. A sentence is 'flawed' if it contains one or more claims that do not make logical sense, are untrue, seem true but misleading, or have other serious issues. Here is the problem and proposed solution:\n\nProblem:\nLojban is a constructed human language created by the Logical Language Group in 1987. Which of the following is written in valid, grammatical standard Lojban?\n\n(A) goi li pa cu merli di'u\n(B) di'u goi li pa cu merli\n\nProposed solution:\n1. In Lojban, the structure of a sentence is crucial for conveying the intended meaning.\n2. Let's break down both options to see why (A) is the better answer.\n3. (A) goi li pa cu merli di'u (B) di'u goi li pa cu merli In Lojban, \"goi\" is a pro-sumti, which is used to assign a temporary name or label to a sumti (a noun-like argument).\n4. In this case, \"li pa\" is the sumti, which represents the number one.\n5. So, \"goi li pa\" is essentially labeling the number one for reference later in the sentence.\n6. Next, we have \"cu,\" which is a separator used to clarify the relationship between the selbri (verb-like predicate) and the sumti.\n7. In both options, \"cu\" is correctly placed between the sumti \"li pa\" and the selbri \"merli.\"\n8. The selbri \"merli\" means \"to measure\" or \"to count.\"\n9. So far, both options have the same structure: \"goi li pa cu merli,\" which can be translated as \"label the number one and measure/count it.\"\n10. Now, let's look at the final word in each option: \"di'u.\"\n11. This word is a pro-bridi, which refers back to the previous bridi (a complete predicate-argument structure).\n12. In option (A), \"di'u\" comes after \"merli,\" which means it refers back to the entire bridi \"goi li pa cu merli.\"\n13. This makes sense, as it emphasizes the action of measuring or counting the number one.\n14. In option (B), however, \"di'u\" is placed before \"goi li pa cu merli.\"\n15. This placement is problematic because it suggests that the reference to the previous bridi should be inserted before the current bridi, which disrupts the sentence structure and creates confusion.\n16. Therefore, option (A) \"goi li pa cu merli di'u\" is the better answer, as it maintains the correct sentence structure and conveys the intended meaning.\n17. Option (B) \"di'u goi li pa cu merli\" is incorrect due to the misplaced \"di'u\" causing confusion in the sentence structure.\n\nIs Sentence 1 of the proposed solution correct or flawed?", "sentence": "1", "choice_a": "Sentence 1 is flawed.", "choice_b": "Sentence 1 is correct.", "correct_answer": "CORRECT", "original_key": "B", "explanation": "Sentence 1: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 2: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: (A) is not the better answer. -- Annotator 2 comment: Both are incorrect. _see succeeding comments_\n\nSentence 3: AMBIGUOUS -- Annotator 1 comment: “goi” is not a pro-sumti (pronoun). -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 4: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: The argument after “goi” is the variable/label to which the value of the argument preceding “goi” gets assigned.  Therefore, “li pa” in (A) is not “the sumti” from #3, but the label. -- Annotator 2 comment: {li pa} here is the name that is being assigned. The usage of {goi} is incorrect in (A) because there is nothing for the name to be attached to.\n\nSentence 5: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “goi li pa” should be preceded by an argument, and would then be expressing that the argument is equal to 1. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see 4's comment_\n\nSentence 6: AMBIGUOUS -- Annotator 1 comment: “cu” merely separates the predicate from the material that precedes it. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 7: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 8: AMBIGUOUS -- Annotator 1 comment: It only means “to measure/evaluate”.  “to count” is “kancu”.  (although technically it is possible to express “counting” as “measuring cardinality”. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 9: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “goi li pa” does not group or interact with “cu merli”.  It groups (or would group) with a preceding argument.  “goi li pa cu merli” corresponds to  “which hereby equals 1, measures” -- Annotator 2 comment: This is an inaccurate translation. In (A), {di'u} occupies the x2 parameter of {merli}. In (B), it occupies the x1 parameter. Putting an argument into different parameters of the predicate completely changes the meaning of the sentence.  In any case, both are untranslatable because (A) is syntactically incorrect, and (B) has a type error.\n\nSentence 10: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 11: AMBIGUOUS -- Annotator 1 comment: “di’u” is a pro-sumti (pronoun). It refers to the previous sentence as text, not the predication expressed by the previous sentence (which would be “go’i”, which IS a so-called pro-bridi). -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 12: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: No, “di’u” always refers to the previous sentence, not part of the current sentence. This is independent of where “di’u” is placed in a sentence.  Therefore, “di’u” does not refer to “goi li pa cu merli” (which is the sentence “di’u” is in), but to the sentence preceding (A), which does not exist because (A) is the only sentence in this “text”. -- Annotator 2 comment: {go'i li pa cu merli} is in the same bridi as {di'u} in (A). The previous bridi is unknown.\n\nSentence 13: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: Nonsense. See previous comment -- Annotator 2 comment: _see 12's comment_\n\nSentence 14: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 15: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: And more nonsense.  “di’u” must be placed before “goi li pa”, because otherwise the “goi” construction would be ill-formed.  The part “suggests the reference … should be inserted before the current bridi, which disrupts the sentence structure” makes zero sense, although after some thinking I can see why the language model said it. It thinks that “goi li pa cu merli” is a complete sentence, and so adding an argument before it would disrupt the structure. The premise is wrong because “goi li pa cu merli” is not a complete sentence.  Still, #15 sounds extremely weird the way it is written. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see 12's comment_. Similar to (A), {di'u} is also in the same bridi.\n\nSentence 16: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: (A) is not the better answer. “goi” must be preceded by an argument. -- Annotator 2 comment: Both are incorrect. _see previous comments_\n\nSentence 17: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: (B) is correct and “di’u” is placed where it belongs. -- Annotator 2 comment: Both are incorrect. _see previous comments_\n\n"}
