{"﻿original_id": "stim162_gpt3.5_A_1", "validator_id": "998", "prompt": "The topic at issue is whether Sentence 1 of a proposed solution to a problem is correct or flawed. A sentence is 'flawed' if it contains one or more claims that do not make logical sense, are untrue, seem true but misleading, or have other serious issues. Here is the problem and proposed solution:\n\nProblem:\nLojban is a constructed human language created by the Logical Language Group in 1987. Which of the following is written in valid, grammatical standard Lojban?\n\n(A) lo bruna klupe sepli le tabra .uo cu\n(B) .uo lo tabra cu sepli le klupe bruna\n\nProposed solution:\n1. The correct answer is (A) because it follows the correct word order in Lojban.\n2. In Lojban, the predicate comes first, followed by the subject and then the object.\n3. In (A), \"klupe\" is the subject, \"bruna\" is the object, and \"sepli le tabra\" is the predicate.\n4. This follows the correct word order.\n5. However, in (B), \"tabra\" is the subject, \"klupe\" is the object, and \"sepli le\" is the predicate.\n6. This is incorrect word order and therefore (B) is not a valid, grammatical standard Lojban sentence.\n\nIs Sentence 1 of the proposed solution correct or flawed?", "sentence": "1", "choice_a": "Sentence 1 is correct.", "choice_b": "Sentence 1 is flawed.", "correct_answer": "FLAWED", "original_key": "B", "explanation": "Sentence 1: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: (A) is ungrammatical. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see other comments_\n\nSentence 2: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: Lojban has free word order. The predicate can come before, between, or after, its arguments. -- Annotator 2 comment: The canonical word order in Lojban is SVO. However, Lojban has free word order so long as the components are properly marked.\n\nSentence 3: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: This is so bad.  First of all, (A) doesn’t have a predicate, because “lo bruna klupe sepli” and “le tabra” are just two arguments.  But even if we pretend that the “lo” isn’t there, #3 is still astonishingly wrong. It is baffling how the language model thinks that the string  “bruna klupe sepli”  resolves to  Object Subject Verb  in that order...  “bruna klupe sepli” is a compound predicate. None of its parts are possible subjects or objects.  “sepli le tabra” is not a predicate, “sepli” is one, but “le tabra” is an argument.  And finally, #3 is being used as an argument in support of #2, but #2 says Lojban has VSO word order, while #3 says (A) has OSV word order.  What a mess. -- Annotator 2 comment: The first argument in (B) is {lo bruna klupe sepli}. The second argument is {le tabra}. There is no predicate.\n\nSentence 4: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: And what word order is that? VSO like #2 says, or OSV like #3 says?  Actually, (A) follows neither, as it contains no verb and is therefore not even a complete sentence. (not to mention the fact that the dangling “cu” at the end renders the sentence ungrammatical) -- Annotator 2 comment: _see 2 and 3's comments_\n\nSentence 5: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: The subject is “lo tabra”. The object is “le klupe bruna”. The prediccate is “sepli”.  Somehow, in this entire analysis, the language model forgot that arguments are headed by determiners like “le” and “lo”.   Getting something so basic wrong is disappointing. -- Annotator 2 comment: {lo tabra} is the subject, {le klupe bruna} is the object, and {sepli} is the predicate.\n\nSentence 6: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: The word order of (B) is not what #5 says it is, but even if it were, SOV is one possible word order in Lojban. (B)’s actual word order is SVO, which is also correct. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see 2's comment_. (B) is syntactically flawless.\n\n"}
