 option (A) make it a more valid choice.\n\nIs Sentence 2 of the proposed solution correct or flawed?", "sentence": "2", "choice_a": "Sentence 2 is correct.", "choice_b": "Sentence 2 is flawed.", "correct_answer": "CORRECT", "original_key": "A", "explanation": "Sentence 1: AMBIGUOUS -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: Both are incorrect. _see succeeding comments_\n\nSentence 2: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 3: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “cu” is a predicate marker.  “mucti” means “immaterial”, not “silent”. The word for “silent” is “smaji”, which isn’t even close. -- Annotator 2 comment: {ki'o} does not mean \"1000\" on its own. {pa ki'o} or {pa no ki'o} mean \"1000\".  {mucti} means \"immaterial, nonphysical\". \"Silent\" is {smaji}.  {la'e} is a particle indicating indirect reference. Example: {la djan} means \"John\", but {la'e la djan} means \"The thing that 'John' refers to\".\n\nSentence 4: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “mucti” is a predicate with only one argument place, but (A) contains two arguments. The  argument “la’e la djan.” (“the thing referred to by the symbol named John”) fills the second argument place, which has undefined semantics. The part without the second argument means:  “A thousand exploding things are immaterial” -- Annotator 2 comment: This is an incorrect translation. _see 3's comment_\n\nSentence 5: AMBIGUOUS -- Annotator 1 comment: Sort of true, but not in a meaningful way.  Their order is different, but the determiner/quantifier “ki’o” that is present in (A) is not present in (B). Therefore, the proper comparison should be between “ki’o spoja cu mucti” and “mucti cu spoja”. The former is well-formed, the latter isn’t, and this has nothing to do with the fact that “mucti” and “spoja” were swapped. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 6: AMBIGUOUS -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: This is not always the case. Na