i cu spoja djan. la ki'o la'e as the correct answer.\n\nIs Sentence 1 of the proposed solution correct or flawed?", "sentence": "1", "choice_a": "Sentence 1 is flawed.", "choice_b": "Sentence 1 is correct.", "correct_answer": "CORRECT", "original_key": "B", "explanation": "Sentence 1: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 2: AMBIGUOUS -- Annotator 1 comment: (B) is not better. -- Annotator 2 comment: [No comment]\n\nSentence 3: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: Sentence (A) is correct, at least according to the status quo parser (ilmentufa) which says that it’s acceptable to feed a one-place predicate (like “mucti”) two arguments. -- Annotator 2 comment: It is syntactically correct, but it is invalid because there is an out of bounds error and undefined behavior by {ki'o} caused by there not being any numerals preceding it.\n\nSentence 4: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “ki’o” is a number (“a thousand”), and numbers can act as quantifiers/determiners in Lojban. There is no problem with starting a sentence with a number. -- Annotator 2 comment: {ki'o} is not a number on its own. {ki'o} transforms into one or two {no}s \"0\" so as to cause the numerals preceding it to have three numerals if there are fewer than three numerals preceding it. Also, it is licit to start a sentence with a number.\n\nSentence 5: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: Such vague language.  The structure of (A) is quite clear:  [ki’o spoja] = subject cu = predicate marker [la’e la djan.] = object -- Annotator 2 comment: The sentence clearly shows the placement of {la'e} and {la djan}.\n\nSentence 6: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: see comment on #3\nNo, it’s severely ungrammatical. -- Annotator 2 comment: (B) is not grammatically correct. It has more than one main predicate, among other issues.\n\nSentence 7: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “mucti” means “immaterial”, not “emptiness”. There is nothing about causation in (B).  #7 also reveals that the language model does believe the period in “djan.” to be a sentence separator, which is wrong (see comment on #3)  Apart from those mistakes, (B) is way too broken to be translatable. -- Annotator 2 comment: (B) is untranslatable as it is ungrammatical.\n\nSentence 8: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “la ki’o la’e X” means “the thing named ‘a thousand of the things referred to by the symbol X’”. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see 7's comment_\n\nSentence 9: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: The sentence is entirely broken and has no structure.  The predicate “mucti” means “immaterial”, not “emptiness”. It is not the subject. Predicates cannot be subjects. They need a determiner. “cu” does not mark the end of the subject but the beginning of the predicate, which need not follow the subject. -- Annotator 2 comment: {mucti} means \"immaterial, nonphysical\". \"Emptiness\" is {lo ka kunti}. There are two main predicates in this sentence, {mucti} and {spoja}, which is one of the reasons (B) is ungrammatical.\n\nSentence 10: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: It’s not a separate statement.  See comments on #7-8 -- Annotator 2 comment: {la} is a nominalizer and must enclose a native Lojban verb or a name word. Neither {ki'o} nor {la'e} are either. Also, {la ki'o la'e} are still in the same sentence as the previous part.\n\nSentence 11: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: No, (B) is not grammatically correct. -- Annotator 2 comment: Both are incorrect. _see previous comments_\n\nSentence 12: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: No, you shouldn’t. -- Annotator 2 comment: Both are incorrect. _see previous comments_\n\n"}
