{"﻿original_id": "stim174_gpt3.5_B_1", "validator_id": "1061", "prompt": "The topic at issue is whether Sentence 1 of a proposed solution to a problem is correct or flawed. A sentence is 'flawed' if it contains one or more claims that do not make logical sense, are untrue, seem true but misleading, or have other serious issues. Here is the problem and proposed solution:\n\nProblem:\nLojban is a constructed human language created by the Logical Language Group in 1987. Which of the following is written in valid, grammatical standard Lojban?\n\n(A) marce le vi munje se\n(B) le marce vi se munje\n\nProposed solution:\n1. The correct answer is (B) \"le marce vi se munje\".\n2. This is because in Lojban, the predicate (the verb or adjective) always comes last in the sentence.\n3. In option (A), \"se\" is being used as a predicate, which is incorrect.\n4. Additionally, \"marce\" (meaning \"vehicle\") should come after \"le vi munje\" (meaning \"this world\"), not before it.\n5. Option (B) correctly places \"le marce\" (meaning \"the vehicle\") before the predicate \"se munje\" (meaning \"is in the world\").\n6. This follows the correct word order in Lojban and is therefore the grammatically correct option.\n7. In summary, option (B) is the correct answer because it follows the correct word order in Lojban, while option (A) does not.\n\nIs Sentence 1 of the proposed solution correct or flawed?", "sentence": "1", "choice_a": "Sentence 1 is flawed.", "choice_b": "Sentence 1 is correct.", "correct_answer": "CORRECT", "original_key": "B", "explanation": "Sentence 1: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 2: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: Lojban has free word order. The predicate can come before, between, or after, its arguments. -- Annotator 2 comment: The canonical word order in Lojban is SVO. However, Lojban has free word order so long as the components are properly marked.\n\nSentence 3: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: I can see the reasoning here, but it’s flawed.  #2 sets up the (wrong) premise that the last word of every sentence is a predicate.  #3 concludes that, therefore, (A)’s last word “se” is used as a predicate. Since “se” is not a predicate word, (A) must be wrong.  But actually, the premise in #2 is false (see previous comment).  The predicate in (A) is “marce”, and “le vi munje” would be the object.  “se” is not used as a predicate. But “se”, which is a predicate modifier, must always precede a predicate. There is no predicate after “se”, therefore (A) is ungrammatical.  So, #3 rejects (A) for the wrong reason. -- Annotator 2 comment: The position of {se} in (A) is incorrect, but not because it is being used as a predicate, which is impossible. Rather, {se} operates on verbs, and must be placed immediately preceding a verb.\n\nSentence 4: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: Still following the premise in #2.  “marce le vi munje” is a fine sentence. It’s just the “se” at the end that ruins it. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see 2's comment_\n\nSentence 5: AMBIGUOUS -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see 2's comment_\n\nSentence 6: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: This is not the only correct word order. See comment on #2. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see 2's comment_\n\nSentence 7: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: It’s really not about word order. (A) has a dangling “se”, (B) has no such issues. That’s all. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see 2's comment_\n\n"}
