as a less coherent structure.\n6. It starts with the name \"la loras\" but then introduces the quantifier \"da'a\" without a clear reference.\n7. The marker \"ku\" is also misplaced, as it should be used to close a previous construct, not to separate the action \"prina mensi\" from the adjective \"fenra\".\n8. This makes the sentence harder to understand and less grammatically correct.\n9. Therefore, I strongly recommend choosing option (A) as the better answer.\n10. It follows the proper Lojban grammar rules and conveys a clearer meaning.\n11. While option (B) may seem similar, its structure is less coherent and more difficult to understand, making it the wrong choice.\n\nIs Sentence 1 of the proposed solution correct or flawed?", "sentence": "1", "choice_a": "Sentence 1 is flawed.", "choice_b": "Sentence 1 is correct.", "correct_answer": "CORRECT", "original_key": "B", "explanation": "Sentence 1: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 2: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: - The quantifier “da’a” means “all but one”. The name article “la” does not take bare quantifiers as complements, so “la da’a” is not the right grouping. “la” heads the entire “la da’a prina mensi fenra” construction.\n\n- “prina” means “to be a print”.\n- “fenra” means “fissure”, not “angry”\n- “prina” means “to be a print”.\n- “fenra” means “fissure”, not “angry”\n- “sumji” means “to be a sum”\n\nYou are not a Lojban expert.\n\n(A) is not the better answer. It is ungrammatical. -- Annotator 2 comment: The LLM is not a Lojban expert.   Both are incorrect. _see succeeding comments_\n\nSentence 3: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 4: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: Ugh.  A more logical flow of words, starting with the terminator “ku” to close a previous construct, but there is no construct that “ku” can close. “ku” is misplaced at the beginning of the sentence.  “ragve le sumji” means “[it] is