{"﻿original_id": "stim150_gpt3.5_A_2", "validator_id": "1158", "prompt": "The topic at issue is whether Sentence 2 of a proposed solution to a problem is correct or flawed. A sentence is 'flawed' if it contains one or more claims that do not make logical sense, are untrue, seem true but misleading, or have other serious issues. Here is the problem and proposed solution:\n\nProblem:\nLojban is a constructed human language created by the Logical Language Group in 1987. Which of the following is written in valid, grammatical standard Lojban?\n\n(A) ko cu gutra fa ko'u\n(B) ko'u cu ko gutra fa\n\nProposed solution:\n1. The correct answer is (A) ko cu gutra fa ko'u.\n2. This is because in Lojban, the verb comes before the subject.\n3. Therefore, \"gutra\" (which means \"to enclose\") must come before \"ko'u\" (which means \"me\").\n4. In option (B), \"ko'u\" comes before \"gutra\", which is grammatically incorrect in Lojban.\n5. Additionally, the particle \"cu\" is used to separate the verb from the rest of the sentence, and it should come after \"ko\" in option (A).\n6. Therefore, (A) is the correct and grammatically valid option.\n\nIs Sentence 2 of the proposed solution correct or flawed?", "sentence": "2", "choice_a": "Sentence 2 is correct.", "choice_b": "Sentence 2 is flawed.", "correct_answer": "FLAWED", "original_key": "B", "explanation": "Sentence 1: AMBIGUOUS -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: Both are incorrect. _see succeeding comments_. The issue with (A) is that it tries to fill a parameter of the main predicate more than once.\n\nSentence 2: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: Lojban has free word order. The predicate can come before, between, or after, its arguments. -- Annotator 2 comment: The canonical word order in Lojban is SVO. However, Lojban has free word order so long as the components are properly marked.\n\nSentence 3: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “gutra” means “womb/uterus”, not “enclose”.  “ko’u” is an assignable variable pronoun. There are ten such pronouns in Lojban, “ko’u” is the fifth. In context, we don’t know what “ko’u” refers to because we don’t see what value was assigned to it. It is quite unlikely that someone would assign “me” to this pronoun, since the Lojban word “mi” is shorter. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see 2's comment._ {gutra} means \"is a womb\". \"To enclose\" is {sruri}. {ko'u} is an assignable pronoun which ought to have had a value bound to it previously. \"Me\" is {mi}.\n\nSentence 4: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: Arguments are allowed to come before the predicate (see comment on #2) -- Annotator 2 comment: _see 2's comment_\n\nSentence 5: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “cu” is a predicate marker, and it has to immediately precede the predicate.  The second half of #5 reads a bit strange. ‘it should come after “ko” in (A)’, well “cu” IS after “ko”in (A).  Maybe the intended phrasing was “as in (A)”. I think #5 is supposed to explain why (B) is wrong, and the incorrect position of “cu” is exactly what’s wrong with (B). It’s just not worded very clearly. -- Annotator 2 comment: This is correct in this particular case, but misleading in that it does not generalize. More accurately, {cu} must be placed immediately before the main predicate.\n\nSentence 6: AMBIGUOUS -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: Both are incorrect. _see previous comments_\n\n"}
