e": "1", "choice_a": "Sentence 1 is flawed.", "choice_b": "Sentence 1 is correct.", "correct_answer": "CORRECT", "original_key": "B", "explanation": "Sentence 1: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 2: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 3: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: Lojban does not have nouns.   Arguments are formed by combining a determiner with a predicate.  The pattern in #3 is inaccurate. Slightly better would be:  le [verb] cu [verb] le [verb]  But more general would be:  [arg] cu [predicate] [arg]  This would sort of adequately describe SVO word order, which is one the most common word orders in Lojban. -- Annotator 2 comment: The canonical word order in Lojban is SVO. However, Lojban has free word order so long as the components are properly marked.\n\nSentence 4: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: Neither (A) nor (B) follow that pattern. Neither sentence has more than one argument. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see 3's comment_\n\nSentence 5: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 6: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 7: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “loldi” is not a noun, it’s a verb (or predicate).  “nicte loldi” is a compound predicate just like “nicte jdika” in (A).   The relative order of “nicte” and “loldi” is not important. The reason why (B) is incorrect is because of the misplaced “cu”, which renders the sentence ungrammatical. -- Annotator 2 comment: The predicates inside a verb phrase can be of any order within the verb phrase and be syntactically valid, though of course that changes the meaning of the verb phrase.\n\nSentence 8: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “le jdika” means “the thing that is decreasing”.  As for “not being connected” to “nicte”, I don’t know how the language model came to that conclusion. Is it because it makes no sense in English or is it something else? Since there is no mention of “cu” anywhere in the argument, I have to assume that the language model doesn’t  understand what’s going on. -- Annotator 2 comment: {le jdika} means \"the decreasing one\". \"The decrease\" is either {le te jdika} \"the amount of decrease\" or {le nu jdika} \"the event of decreasing\"\n\nSentence 9: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 10: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 11: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: You are not a Lojban expert. -- Annotator 2 comment: The LLM is not a Lojban expert.\n\n"}
