{"﻿original_id": "stim159_gpt3.5_A_1", "validator_id": "1364", "prompt": "The topic at issue is whether Sentence 1 of a proposed solution to a problem is correct or flawed. A sentence is 'flawed' if it contains one or more claims that do not make logical sense, are untrue, seem true but misleading, or have other serious issues. Here is the problem and proposed solution:\n\nProblem:\nLojban is a constructed human language created by the Logical Language Group in 1987. Which of the following is written in valid, grammatical standard Lojban?\n\n(A) le go'i sepli drani cu pinca le milxe\n(B) milxe go'i le drani le cu sepli pinca\n\nProposed solution:\n1. The correct answer is (A) because it follows the correct Lojban word order.\n2. In Lojban, the predicate comes first, followed by the subject and then the object.\n3. In (A), \"pinca\" is the predicate, \"le go'i sepli drani\" is the subject, and \"le milxe\" is the object.\n4. In (B), the word order is incorrect, with \"milxe\" coming first, followed by \"go'i le drani le cu sepli\" and then \"pinca\".\n5. This is not a valid Lojban sentence structure and therefore (B) is incorrect.\n6. Furthermore, in (A), the use of \"sepli\" to mean \"separate\" is more appropriate than using \"le drani le cu sepli\" in (B).\n7. \"Sepli\" is a more concise and clear way to express the idea of separation in Lojban.\n8. Therefore, (A) is the correct answer and (B) is incorrect due to its incorrect word order and less appropriate use of vocabulary.\n\nIs Sentence 1 of the proposed solution correct or flawed?", "sentence": "1", "choice_a": "Sentence 1 is correct.", "choice_b": "Sentence 1 is flawed.", "correct_answer": "CORRECT", "original_key": "A", "explanation": "Sentence 1: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 2: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: Lojban has free word order. The predicate can come before, between, or after, its arguments. -- Annotator 2 comment: The canonical word order in Lojban is SVO. However, Lojban has free word order so long as the components are properly marked.\n\nSentence 3: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 4: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: The groupings in #4 make no sense.   “milxe” forms a compound predicate with “go’i”, and “sepli” forms a compound predicate with “pinca”. “le drani” is an argument and stands alone:  [milxe go’i] [le drani] [le] cu [sepli pinca]  The issue is not that the word order deviates from SVO, but that the sentence has two predicates (a sentence can only have one), and that the second “le” is missing its complement. -- Annotator 2 comment: _See 2's comment_. The issue with (B) is that {cu} must be placed immediately before the main predicate. In {le cu drani}, {cu} is not placed before the main predicate.\n\nSentence 5: AMBIGUOUS -- Annotator 1 comment: I wonder what the LLM thinks (B)’s word order is. #4 doesn’t say, but it might be thinking it’s OSV, because “milxe” was part of the object in (A), and “go’i” and “drani” were part of the subject.  See the previous comment for the real reason why (B) is invalid. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 6: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “sepli” is more correct than “le drani le cu sepli”, sure, but that’s because the latter is ungrammatical. Calling the former “more appropriate” is a bit weird. It’s not even a good comparison, because “sepli” in (A) is just a simple predicate, while “le drani le cu sepli” is a complex jumbled sequence of words.  It’s also not clear why “sepli” is the one word that the LLM thought needed to be translated. It is hardly the most important word in (A). #6 seems really random overall. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see 4's comment_\n\nSentence 7: AMBIGUOUS -- Annotator 1 comment: Well yes, because “sepli” means “to be separate”, while the mess in (B) you arbitrarily compared it to contains a bunch of additional words.  The word “sepli” appears in both (A) and (B) and means the same in both, so this is a really bad reason for preferring (A). -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 8: AMBIGUOUS -- Annotator 1 comment: Bad reasoning from start to finish. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\n"}
