{"﻿original_id": "stim148_gpt3.5_A_1", "validator_id": "1406", "prompt": "The topic at issue is whether Sentence 1 of a proposed solution to a problem is correct or flawed. A sentence is 'flawed' if it contains one or more claims that do not make logical sense, are untrue, seem true but misleading, or have other serious issues. Here is the problem and proposed solution:\n\nProblem:\nLojban is a constructed human language created by the Logical Language Group in 1987. Which of the following is written in valid, grammatical standard Lojban?\n\n(A) lei ba stidi cu ckape pinca ko'a\n(B) stidi cu ko'a lei ckape ba pinca\n\nProposed solution:\n1. The correct answer is (A) lei ba stidi cu ckape pinca ko'a.\n2. This is because in Lojban, the order of the sentence is determined by the logical relationships between the words, rather than by the usual subject-verb-object order found in English.\n3. In this sentence, \"lei ba stidi\" is the subject, \"cu\" is the indicator of the main verb, \"ckape\" is the verb, and \"pinca ko'a\" is the object.\n4. This order makes logical sense because it first identifies the subject, then the verb, and finally the object.\n5. On the other hand, (B) stidi cu ko'a lei ckape ba pinca has the verb \"stidi\" before the subject \"ko'a\", which is not the correct order in Lojban.\n6. Additionally, \"lei ckape ba pinca\" is not a valid object because it is missing the \"cu\" indicator of the main verb.\n7. Therefore, (B) is not a grammatically correct sentence in Lojban.\n8. In conclusion, (A) is the correct answer because it follows the logical order of Lojban sentence structure and has a valid subject, verb, and object.\n\nIs Sentence 1 of the proposed solution correct or flawed?", "sentence": "1", "choice_a": "Sentence 1 is correct.", "choice_b": "Sentence 1 is flawed.", "correct_answer": "CORRECT", "original_key": "A", "explanation": "Sentence 1: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 2: AMBIGUOUS -- Annotator 1 comment: I don’t know what this means.  Lojban has free word order. You can move arguments around freely as long as their relative order remains the same. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 3: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: The verb is “ckape pinca”, not just “ckape”. Two adjecent predicates always form a compound predicate.  The object is just “ko’a”. -- Annotator 2 comment: {ckape pinca} is the verb. It is a tanru (verb phrase) and must be considered as one unit. {ko'a} is the object.\n\nSentence 4: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: This is a strange continuation from #2, which seemed to say that Lojban’s word order doesn’t necessarily follow SVO word order, but #4 says (A) makes logical sense because it has SVO word order. Confusing. -- Annotator 2 comment: The canonical word order in Lojban is SVO. However, Lojban has free word order so long as the components are properly marked.\n\nSentence 5: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: Now this seems to say that VSO word order is wrong (which it isn’t).  However, it’s not right to call “ko’a” the subject and move on. (B) is heavily jumbled and contains multiple syntactic impossibilities:  - the predicate marker “cu” precedes the pronoun “ko’a” - the sentence has two predicates (“stidi” and “pinca”)  #5 is way too naïve in its analysis, checking only for SVO word order and missing the real issues -- Annotator 2 comment: _see 4's comment_\n\nSentence 6: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “lei ckape ba pinca” is not a valid object (it’s a full sentence, pretty much), but the assumption that is must be the object because #5 already identified a verb and a subject, is false, and too simplistic. Sentences can contain a number of other things besides a verb, a subject, and an object. For example, prepositional phrases, and various free modifiers. If “lei ckape ba pinca” happened to be one of those, #6 would probably still have complained that isn’t a well-formed object, because the LLM can only think in terms of SVO and knows no other parts of speech, even though it might not cause them sentence to be ungrammatical. -- Annotator 2 comment: It is not a valid object, but in the sense that in this case, the scope of {lei} is terminated by {ba}. Therefore, {lei ckape} is an argument, and {ba pinca} is a predicate. This causes a syntax error, because then there are two main predicates in (B), {stidi} and {ba pinca}.\n\nSentence 7: AMBIGUOUS -- Annotator 1 comment: (B) is ungrammatical because it has two predicates and “cu” is misplaced. It’s that simple and has nothing to do with SVO word order. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 8: AMBIGUOUS -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see 4's comment_\n\n"}
