 are two main selbri in the sentence, {stidi} and {pinca}.\n\nSentence 6: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: #6 seems to have invented a new grammar rule. I can see what it did even though it’s completely wrong.  It reads “stidi cu …” as “suggesting causes …”, giving the predicate marker “cu” a causative reading (creative, but wrong)  In proper Lojban, this would be  “lo nu stidi cu rinka lo nu …” “the event of suggesting causes the event of ...”  and then the rest of the sentence would actually fall into place.  But yeah that’s not how Lojban works.  (and the translations for “stidi”  (“student”) and “pinca” (“urine”) are still wrong) -- Annotator 2 comment: (B) is untranslatable as it is ungrammatical. _see 5's comment_\n\nSentence 7: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: It’s ungrammatical. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see 6's comment_\n\nSentence 8: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: (B) is wrong. It does not follow Lojban grammar. It has two predicates and a misplaced predicate marker “cu”. (A) is correct. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see preceding comments_\n\n"}
