 word order in Lojban is SVO. However, Lojban has free word order so long as the components are properly marked.\n\nSentence 3: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “tapla” means “to be a tile”, not “to be an apple”. And technically it’s not “tapla” that’s the subject but “le tapla”.  “mu sabji” means “five providers”, as in, five sources that provide a resource or commodity.  Now, #3 claims that “lebna” precedes the subject in (A), but that’s visibly false. The subject “le tapla” comes first in the sentence, making the sentence SVO! What a weird mistake, and how weird that this happens so often. -- Annotator 2 comment: In (A), {le tapla} comes first, followed by {lebna} and then {mu sabji}.  {lebna} is translated correctly. However, {tapla} means \"tile\" and {mu sabji} means \"five suppliers\". \"The apple\" and \"my vegetables\" are {le plise} and {lo mi stagi} respectively.\n\nSentence 4: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: It’s SVO, not VSO like you said.  But both SVO and VSO are possible word orders in Lojban. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see previous comments_\n\nSentence 5: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 6: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “lebna” comes first? Are you sure? It’s the fourth word of the sentence!  How in the world is “sabji” the object? It forms a compound predicate with “lebna”, and together they are the complement of the determiner “le”. How is “tapla” the subject? It doesn’t have a determiner, so it can’t be an argument. And you say that “tapla” comes after “sabji”, even though “tapla” is the very first word of the sentence.  What is going on here?  I have no words. -- Annotator 2 comment: In (B), {tapla} comes first, followed by {lebna} and then {sabji}.\n\nSentence 7: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: The LLM seems to think that the problem is that (B) has VOS word order, which differs from the VSO word order that #2 demands.  Now, (B) is neither plausibly VOS, nor would VOS be incorrect.  For reference. the actual structure of (B) is this:  [tapla] [mu le lebna sabji] cu [verb] [object] cu  Since the argument after “tapla” does not carry the subject marker “fa”, it must be the object, or second argument. The “cu” at the end of the sentence is misplaced and is what causes the sentence to be ungrammatical. The LLM talks a whole lot about word order but keeps missing these simple details. -- Annotator 2 comment: This is correct, but for the wrong reasons. (B) is incorrect because {cu} is not placed immediately before the main predicate.\n\nSentence 8: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 9: AMBIGUOUS -- Annotator 1 comment: As you have showns, you can choose (A) while being utterly clueless at the same time. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\n"}
