sing its complement, but this is allowed by the grammar. This does not make the sentence ambiguous or incorrect. The reason why (B) is ungrammatical is that the predicate marker  “cu” is followed by a determiner phrase. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 9: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: (I’m assume the “test-taker” is something that appeared in the prompt, otherwise it would be super random.) “fi” does not refer, see comment on #6. -- Annotator 2 comment: {fi} here is attached to {ko}, which refers to the intended recipient of the speech.\n\nSentence 10: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 11: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: (B) is ungrammatical. The use of “fi” is not ambiguous. There is no predicate in (B), but the predicate marker “cu” is followed by a determiner phrase, which is why (B) is ungrammatical. -- Annotator 2 comment: _See 2's comment._\n\n"}
