f {do} \"you\".\n\nSentence 9: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: (A) is fine. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see 8's comment_\n\nSentence 10: CORRECT -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 11: AMBIGUOUS -- Annotator 1 comment: No problem. -- Annotator 2 comment: It is not. {fi} must be immediately followed by an argument, and {cu} is not one.\n\nSentence 12: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: “fi cu” is not a preposition.  “fi” is a preposition-like object marker whose complement was omitted in (B), which is allowed by the grammar.   “cu” is a always just a predicate marker, which introduces the main predicate of a clause. It cannot be followed by an argument.   Since “cu” is followed by the determiner phrase “le jukpa broda”, the sentence is rendered ungrammatical. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see 11's comment_\n\nSentence 13: AMBIGUOUS -- Annotator 1 comment: No, “broda” is part of “le jukpa broda” (see comment on #3).  Therefore, (B) doesn’t have a predicate.  “ko” is not a command, but a pronoun which turns the sentence into a command. -- Annotator 2 comment: No problem.\n\nSentence 14: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: The structure is ungrammatical because the predicate marker “cu” is followed by an argument instead of a predicate. -- Annotator 2 comment: It does not. I would translate \"Do the action (broda) with the cook\" as {.i ko broda ka'ai le jukpa}.\n\nSentence 15: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: (B) is not the correct answer. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see previous comments_\n\nSentence 16: FLAWED -- Annotator 1 comment: It’s not valid. It’s not grammatical. -- Annotator 2 comment: _see previous comments_\n\n"}
